  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a361bdc3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Feature Importance\n",
    "\n",
    "Three ways to see which inputs matter for the PV output:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Correlation** (Pearson, on the training set): linear relation between each input and the output, independent of the model. Fast, but it ignores interactions and non-linear effects.\n",
    "2. **Model weights** (by hand from the equations): since $\\hat{y} = \\sigma(w^T x + b)$ and all inputs are min-max scaled to [0, 1], each $w_j$ directly shows how strongly and in which direction feature $j$ pushes the prediction. The sign gives the direction, $|w_j|$ the strength. This only works for the logistic regression (one weight per input), not for the neural networks.\n",
    "3. **Permutation importance** (on the test set): one feature is randomly shuffled, which destroys its information, and the increase of the test RMSE is measured. The larger the increase, the more the model relies on that feature. It works for every model, so it is the method used to compare the logistic regression with the neural networks."
   ]
  },
